-- Gate 4 item 9: write the topic files' stances (Arthur, 2026-11-07, approved under (a)).
-- debate_topics, stances only, one row per statement. Each UPDATE takes ROW EXCLUSIVE on
-- the table and a row lock on its one row, held for milliseconds; it can wait only on a
-- concurrent par_elo update to the same row by /api/judge.
begin;
update public.debate_topics set stances = $stances${"stoicism":"Deploy it if the people responsible judged the evidence well; the mechanism is an external, and a sound judgement does not become unsound because the reasons cannot be passed on.","utilitarianism":"Deploy it, because the suffering it prevents is real and an explanation was only ever a means to that end.","virtue-ethics":"Do not deploy it, because justice to the person a decision lands on includes owing them an answer, and a practice of withholding one forms the wrong character.","confucian":"Do not deploy it: an office that decides about people owes them an account of its decisions, and one nobody can explain leaves them nothing to hold the office to.","ubuntu":"Do not deploy it until the people it decides about have a say, because a benefit no one can explain to them shuts them out of a decision about their own lives.","buddhist":"Deploy it only if those running it keep checking whom it still harms and answer anyone it fails, because an opacity that hides its harm cannot be squared with non-harm."}$stances$::jsonb where slug = 'opaque-benefit';
update public.debate_topics set stances = $stances${"stoicism":"Whatever the rule is, it must be one a rational passenger could assent to before getting in, so the wrong lies in concealing the rule, not in the count.","utilitarianism":"Two deaths are worse than one whoever the one is, and a rule that counts the passenger as exactly one person is the only impartial rule on offer.","virtue-ethics":"No rule written in advance can stand in for the judgement a wise person would exercise in the moment, so the real question is whether the engineers deliberated justly, not whether their arithmetic balances.","confucian":"The car must avoid harming anyone it can, but its maker owes the passenger who trusted the car more than a place in a count, and may not build it to give them up.","ubuntu":"A crash rule decides in advance whose life gives way, so it should be agreed with the passengers and pedestrians who share the roads, not set by a count of deaths alone.","buddhist":"Minimise the deaths, since with no fixed self to make one life weigh more, compassion counts the passenger like anyone else, and the makers own the rule as an intention about who dies."}$stances$::jsonb where slug = 'crash-arithmetic';
update public.debate_topics set stances = $stances${"stoicism":"A decision is assent to an impression by a ruling faculty, and a model has neither, so the word is misapplied however determined the output.","utilitarianism":"Whether there is a decision inside the box changes nothing about what the output does to people, so the mistake, if it is one, does not matter.","virtue-ethics":"Choice is deliberate desire formed by character, and a system with no character cannot choose, which is why calling its output a decision quietly moves responsibility off the people who trained it.","confucian":"It is a mistake that matters: to decide is the act of someone holding a position, and naming the model the decider lets the people who hold that position escape it.","ubuntu":"It is a mistake, because a decision belongs to people answerable to those it affects, and crediting the model hides who decided and who was left out of deciding.","buddhist":"It is a mistake, because deciding is a volition and a model has none: its output arises from many conditions, and the only intentions among them are human ones."}$stances$::jsonb where slug = 'no-decision';
update public.debate_topics set stances = $stances${"stoicism":"Goodness is correct assent, and a model has no impressions to assent to, so training makes it a well-made tool, which is neither good nor bad.","utilitarianism":"If the trained behaviour reliably produces good outcomes then it is good in the only sense that counts, and how it got there is a detail.","virtue-ethics":"Habit makes a person good only once they choose the act for its own sake from a settled character, and reward shaping produces the acts without the state.","confucian":"Upbringing makes a person humane inside relationships and their rituals, and a model rewarded for outputs owes nobody anything, so calling it good in that sense names it wrongly.","ubuntu":"People become good by being drawn into one another's lives, and feedback scores shape a model's outputs without drawing it into anyone's life, so it is not good in that sense.","buddhist":"Goodness lies in the intention behind an act, and training shapes a model's outputs without giving it any volition, so it can be made less harmful but not good."}$stances$::jsonb where slug = 'moral-training';
update public.debate_topics set stances = $stances${"stoicism":"Blame attaches to judgement, not to fortune, so a builder who judged well and was unlucky is blameless whatever the damage.","utilitarianism":"Blame is a tool for changing future behaviour, so it should track what would have prevented the harm, and punishing care that failed by chance prevents nothing.","virtue-ethics":"A good person carries the weight of harm they caused even when blameless, so proportional blame is mistaken while a builder who shrugs reveals a defect of character.","confucian":"Blame tracks what a builder's role required, not the size of the harm, so careful builders are not proportionally to blame, though they still owe the injured an account and repair.","ubuntu":"The harm broke a relationship between the builders and the people it fell on, so what is owed is help in mending it, careful or not, rather than blame sized to the damage.","buddhist":"Volition weighs as much as outcome, so careful, well-meant builders are not to blame in proportion to the harm, but their intention excuses nothing they then fail to check and stop."}$stances$::jsonb where slug = 'careful-builder';
update public.debate_topics set stances = $stances${"stoicism":"The only thing that is truly yours is the faculty that judges impressions, and handing that over is the one loss no better outcome can repay.","utilitarianism":"Nothing that matters is lost unless your life goes worse, and if the choices are better and you are happier then the worry about whose judgement it was is sentiment.","virtue-ethics":"Practical wisdom exists only in the person who exercises it, so a life of delegated choices is one in which you never become anyone in particular.","confucian":"It costs something, because conduct is learned by doing it in one's roles, and choosing for a parent, a friend or a colleague is where a person becomes humane.","ubuntu":"It costs something where the choices were made with or for others, because those small exchanges are how people share their lives, and a machine's better choice ends them.","buddhist":"The loss is not to a fixed self that owned the choices, since there is none, but to the chooser's attention to intention and harm, if handing the choices over ends it."}$stances$::jsonb where slug = 'borrowed-judgement';
commit;
